<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<component id="org.eclipse.equinox.p2.ui" version="2">
    <resource path="src/org/eclipse/equinox/internal/p2/ui/actions/PropertyDialogAction.java" type="org.eclipse.equinox.internal.p2.ui.actions.PropertyDialogAction">
        <filter comment="p2's PropertyDialogAction intentionally extends org.eclipse.ui.dialogs.PropertyDialogAction to customize the properties dialog for p2 elements" id="571473929">
            <message_arguments>
                <message_argument value="PropertyDialogAction"/>
                <message_argument value="PropertyDialogAction"/>
            </message_arguments>
        </filter>
    </resource>
    <resource path="src/org/eclipse/equinox/internal/p2/ui/actions/UpdateAction.java" type="org.eclipse.equinox.internal.p2.ui.actions.UpdateAction">
        <filter comment="UpdateAction.performAction intentionally creates a RemediationOperation, which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI" id="640708718">
            <message_arguments>
                <message_argument value="RemediationOperation(ProvisioningSession, IProfileChangeRequest)"/>
                <message_argument value="UpdateAction"/>
            </message_arguments>
        </filter>
        <filter comment="UpdateAction's runModal intentionally calls ProfileChangeOperation.resolveModal(IProgressMonitor), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI" id="640712815">
            <message_arguments>
                <message_argument value="ProfileChangeOperation"/>
                <message_argument value="UpdateAction"/>
                <message_argument value="resolveModal(IProgressMonitor)"/>
            </message_arguments>
        </filter>
        <filter comment="Please look at the current branch, I have some changes there. Please check the screenshot, which is what I have implemented. &#10;&#10;Please check the screenshot, do you have any idea why I got this error&#10;▸ 11 earlier steps&#10;EXECUTE&#10;grep -n &quot;noimplement&quot; bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.repository/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/repository/metadata/IMetadataRepositoryManager.java; grep -n &quot;class MetadataRepositoryManager&quot; -A1 bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.metadata.repository/src/org/eclipse/equinox/internal/p2/metadata/repository/MetadataRepositoryManager.java; git log -3 --format='%h %ad %s' -- bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.repository/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/repository/artifact/IArtifactRepositoryManager.java bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.repository/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/repository/metadata/IMetadataRepositoryManager.java&#10;The error doesn't come from your changes, as far as I can tell. I haven't seen the build log or the tool that produced the report, so why it shows up now is my best guess.&#10;&#10;What the error is. It's a PDE API Tools usage warning (USAGE / ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT, severity Normal). The class ArtifactRepositoryManager implements IArtifactRepositoryManager. That interface is tagged @noimplement in IArtifactRepositoryManager.java. The class sits in a different bundle (p2.artifact.repository) from the interface (p2.repository), so API Tools flags it.&#10;&#10;Why I don't think you caused it.&#10;&#10;Your branch install_remediation_page_improvements_1138 has two commits on top of origin/master: 5775f21e0 (remediation page) and 91a40971d (the .api_filters fix for p2.ui).&#10;Neither commit touches p2.artifact.repository or p2.repository.&#10;Nothing in the code you changed relates to ArtifactRepositoryManager.&#10;MetadataRepositoryManager has the same pattern, since IMetadataRepositoryManager is also @noimplement. It isn't in your report, so I don't know why only the artifact one is flagged.&#10;&quot;Age 1&quot; most likely means the warning first appeared in this build. That would happen if the report was compared against a different reference build, or if API analysis ran on that bundle this time. I can't confirm either from the repo.&#10;What to do. Add an API filter in bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.artifact.repository/.settings/.api_filters for the illegal-implement problem on ArtifactRepositoryManager. That bundle has no .api_filters file today. Do it only if the build actually fails on it. Otherwise it's a warning that exists on origin/master too.&#10;&#10;It may be easier to tell from the failing build's log, if you paste it or tell me which job produced the report, whether this is a hard failure or just a warning in the report. I can also generate the filter, but the exact filter id depends on your API Tools version. A quick fix in Eclipse is to right-click the problem in the Problems view and choose &quot;Create API filter&quot;.&#10;&#10;which one should I pick&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ArtifactRepositoryManager'&quot;.&#10;&#10;Commented filter: it adds an entry to .settings/.api_filters and asks you for a comment. Enter something like &quot;Internal default implementation of a @noimplement interface&quot;. Reviewers can then see why the warning is suppressed.&#10;Plain filter: it does the same without a comment, so use it only if you don't need the explanation.&#10;Configure usage problem severity: this changes the severity for the whole project or workspace. That's too broad for one known case, so skip it.&#10;Do this in p2.artifact.repository. The filter is only worth committing if the build actually fails on this warning. Otherwise it's unrelated to your remediation work, and I'd leave it out of this branch.&#10;&#10;what would be the filtername&#10;That field is a free-text comment, not a filter name. Type a short reason, for example:&#10;&#10;ArtifactRepositoryManager is the internal default implementation of IArtifactRepositoryManager&#10;&#10;Then click Proceed. The comment is saved in .settings/.api_filters next to the filter.&#10;&#10;what would be the filtername&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class ArtifactRequest|interface IArtifactRequest|@noimplement&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;There's no filter name to enter. The dialog only asks for a comment, and the name is generated automatically.&#10;&#10;This is the same situation as ArtifactRepositoryManager. ArtifactRequest is the internal implementation in p2.artifact.repository. It implements IArtifactRequest, which is @noimplement in p2.repository (line 27).&#10;&#10;Choose &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter&quot; again. For the comment, type:&#10;&#10;ArtifactRequest is the internal default implementation of IArtifactRequest&#10;&#10;This is again an existing upstream warning, not something your remediation changes caused.&#10;&#10;hope adding api filters would be the perfect fix, what would be my message&#10;▸ 1 earlier step&#10;READ&#10;Reading file /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.artifact.repository/.settings/.api_filters&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;Adding the filters should clear these two warnings, but I can't promise it's the complete fix.&#10;&#10;The file .api_filters currently has only the ArtifactRepositoryManager filter. The ArtifactRequest one isn't in it yet, so apply that quick fix too.&#10;The file is untracked, so run git add on it.&#10;The build may report more of these ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT warnings in the same bundle. Rebuild and check after committing.&#10;Suggested commit message:&#10;&#10;CopyInsert&#10;Add API filters for internal implementations of @noimplement interfaces&#10;&#10;ArtifactRepositoryManager and ArtifactRequest are the internal default&#10;implementations of IArtifactRepositoryManager and IArtifactRequest.&#10;Both interfaces are marked @noimplement for clients, which makes API&#10;Tools report ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT usage problems for these classes.&#10;Filter the problems, since the implementations are intentional.&#10;If you'd rather not mix this into the remediation branch, make it a separate commit or a separate PR.&#10;&#10;I have a confusion. Like we added the api filters for the ArtifactRepositoryManager, would the same approach fix the issues in the ArtifactRequest?&#10;Yes. Both are the same kind of problem, so the same approach works.&#10;&#10;Same cause: both classes are internal implementations of an interface tagged @noimplement. ArtifactRepositoryManager implements IArtifactRepositoryManager, and ArtifactRequest implements IArtifactRequest.&#10;Same bundle: both classes are in p2.artifact.repository, so both filters go in the same .api_filters file.&#10;Same result: the filter tells API Tools to ignore that one problem on that one class.&#10;Each problem needs its own filter, because a filter matches a specific class and message. The existing ArtifactRepositoryManager filter won't cover ArtifactRequest. Apply the &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ArtifactRequest'&quot; quick fix, and the file will get a second &lt;resource&gt; entry for it.&#10;&#10;After that, rebuild and check whether any other classes in the bundle show the same warning. Each would need its own filter.&#10;&#10;so what would be my message for filter comment&#10;Use this as the filter comment for ArtifactRequest:&#10;&#10;ArtifactRequest is the internal default implementation of IArtifactRequest&#10;&#10;It follows the same wording as the ArtifactRepositoryManager comment.&#10;&#10;hope adding an api filter would fix this issue. if soe provde a filter comment&#10;▸ 1 earlier step&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class CompositeArtifactRepository|interface ICompositeRepository|@noimplement&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;Yes, a filter should fix this one too. CompositeArtifactRepository is another internal class in p2.artifact.repository. It implements ICompositeRepository&lt;IArtifactKey&gt;, which is @noimplement in p2.repository (line 31). That is the same pattern as the other two.&#10;&#10;Use the &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'CompositeArtifactRepository'&quot; quick fix, with this comment:&#10;&#10;CompositeArtifactRepository is the internal default implementation of ICompositeRepository&#10;&#10;This is the third warning of this kind in the bundle, and more may follow. After you've added these, rebuild and check which ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT warnings remain. If many more appear, ask whether the build really fails on these warnings. If it doesn't, you could leave them alone instead of filtering each one.&#10;&#10;hoep api filters fix it. if yes, provide a filter comment&#10;▸ 2 earlier steps&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.artifact.repository/src for &quot;class SimpleArtifactRepository&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;Yes, a filter should fix this one too. SimpleArtifactRepository implements IFileArtifactRepository, which is @noimplement in p2.repository (line 22). It's the same pattern as the first three, and the class is in the same bundle.&#10;&#10;Use the &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'SimpleArtifactRepository'&quot; quick fix, with this comment:&#10;&#10;SimpleArtifactRepository is the internal default implementation of IFileArtifactRepository&#10;&#10;Your .api_filters already has the first three filters: ArtifactRepositoryManager, ArtifactRequest and CompositeArtifactRepository. This will be the fourth.&#10;&#10;I haven't run the build, so I can't confirm the filters clear the warnings. These are all the same kind of warning, so I'd rebuild after this one and see what's left. If another one appears, add it the same way.&#10;&#10;StrongPool&lt;T&gt; implements non-API interface IPool&lt;T&gt;&#10;&#10;suggest an api filter comment&#10;▸ 2 earlier steps&#10;EXECUTE&#10;grep -n &quot;org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core;&quot; -B1 -A3 bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core/META-INF/MANIFEST.MF; ls -a bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core/.settings; sed -n 20,35p bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/core/StrongPool.java; ls bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/core/ | head -30&#10;I'd be more careful with this one than with the earlier four.&#10;&#10;It's a different kind of warning. The earlier ones said &quot;illegally implements&quot;, meaning an internal class implements a @noimplement interface. This one says StrongPool&lt;T&gt; &quot;implements non-API interface&quot; IPool&lt;T&gt;, which is an API leak-type message.&#10;&#10;It also looks odd to me for these reasons:&#10;&#10;StrongPool and IPool are in the same package, org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core.&#10;That package is exported in the manifest without x-internal or x-friends.&#10;IPool is only @noimplement, which restricts clients from implementing it. It doesn't make the interface non-API.&#10;Your branch doesn't touch p2.core.&#10;So I can't say why API Tools treats IPool as non-API here. I haven't seen the full report entry, and I can't confirm a filter will clear it.&#10;&#10;If you still want to filter it, use the same quick fix on StrongPool, in the p2.core bundle. Use this as the comment:&#10;&#10;StrongPool is the default implementation of IPool, which is intended for use by p2 and not for client implementation&#10;&#10;If you can show me the full report entry (category, type, file and line), I can say more.&#10;&#10;this screenshot give you more information&#10;EXECUTE&#10;ls -a bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core | head -20; grep -rn &quot;IPool\|StrongPool&quot; bundles/*/.settings/.api_filters | head; git log -5 --format='%h %ad %s' -- bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core/META-INF/MANIFEST.MF&#10;That screenshot adds one useful detail. The type is API_LEAK in USAGE, so it's a different problem from the earlier ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT ones. API Tools says that an API class (StrongPool) exposes a type (IPool) that it doesn't treat as API.&#10;&#10;I still can't explain why API Tools sees IPool as non-API. In the source, both types are in the same exported package, org.eclipse.equinox.p2.core, and IPool only has @noimplement. Nothing in the repo points to a cause, and your branch doesn't touch p2.core. So this is a pre-existing warning, and I haven't confirmed that a filter clears it.&#10;&#10;If you decide to filter it, use the same quick fix on StrongPool, in the p2.core bundle. A comment that matches the warning:&#10;&#10;StrongPool is the default implementation of IPool. IPool is intended to be used through StrongPool and WeakPool, so the leak is intentional.&#10;&#10;I'd still keep these filters out of your remediation-page branch. They are unrelated to your change, and p2.core is a different bundle from the ones you edited. Add them only if the build fails on these warnings. If it does, put them in a separate commit or PR.&#10;&#10;provide filter comment&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class PrettyQuery|interface IQuery&lt;|^ \* @noimplement&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;That search was broader than I needed, but it confirms the cause. PrettyQuery is an internal class in p2.director.app that implements IQuery&lt;T&gt;. IQuery is @noimplement in p2.metadata (line 32). That's the same ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT pattern as the artifact repository ones.&#10;&#10;Use the &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'PrettyQuery'&quot; quick fix, in the p2.director.app bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;PrettyQuery is an internal wrapper that intentionally implements IQuery&#10;&#10;This is another pre-existing warning in a bundle your branch doesn't touch. If you add the filter, keep it out of the remediation-page commit.&#10;&#10;filter comment&#10;EXECUTE&#10;grep -n &quot;newValue\|noreference&quot; bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.metadata/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/metadata/IRequirementChange.java; sed -n 848,852p bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.director/src/org/eclipse/equinox/internal/p2/director/Projector.java&#10;This is a different warning type. It's ILLEGAL_REFERENCE, not ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT. Projector calls IRequirementChange.newValue(). That method is marked @noreference in IRequirementChange.java, so clients shouldn't call it. Projector is p2's own director code, so the call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Use the &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'Projector'&quot; quick fix, in the p2.director bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;Projector is p2's internal resolver and intentionally calls IRequirementChange.newValue(), which is marked @noreference for clients&#10;&#10;Projector references newValue() in more than one place, since the code calls it twice on lines 850 and 851. The filter may cover only one reference, so rebuild and check whether another warning remains. It's again a pre-existing warning in a bundle your branch doesn't touch.&#10;&#10;this is the routecause of the problem&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.director/src/org/eclipse/equinox/internal/p2/director/Projector.java for &quot;mergeRequirements|newValue\(\)&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;The screenshot confirms the cause. The warning is on a call inside the method mergeRequirements(IInstallableUnit, IInstallableUnitPatch). That's why the quick fix names the method.&#10;&#10;Scope of the filter: the filter is for that method, not for the whole Projector class. In the earlier answer I said the filter was &quot;for 'Projector'&quot;, and that was wrong. Line 533 is only where the method is called.&#10;Several calls, one filter: newValue() is called at lines 851, 852, 861 and 862, all inside mergeRequirements. The method-level filter should cover all of them.&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'mergeRequirements(IInstallableUnit, IInstallableUnitPatch)'&quot;. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;Projector.mergeRequirements intentionally calls IRequirementChange.newValue(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own resolver&#10;&#10;Add it in the p2.director bundle.&#10;&#10;another one&#10;This is the same kind of warning in the same method, but it needs its own filter. The earlier filter was for IRequirementChange.newValue(). This one is for IRequirementChange.applyOn(), and a filter only matches its own method name in the message. applyOn() is also marked @noreference in IRequirementChange.java.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'mergeRequirements(IInstallableUnit, IInstallableUnitPatch)'&quot; again, in the p2.director bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;Projector.mergeRequirements intentionally calls IRequirementChange.applyOn(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own resolver&#10;&#10;Each different @noreference method called in mergeRequirements is reported separately, so you'll probably need one filter per method. Rebuild afterwards to see what's left.&#10;&#10;another one&#10;This one is in a different method, safeMatch(IRequirement[], IRequirementChange, int), so it needs its own filter. It's for IRequirementChange.matches(IRequiredCapability), which is another @noreference method.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'safeMatch(IRequirement[], IRequirementChange, int)'&quot;, in the p2.director bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;Projector.safeMatch intentionally calls IRequirementChange.matches(IRequiredCapability), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own resolver&#10;&#10;how to fix this&#10;Use the first option: &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'StrongPool'&quot;. Do it in the p2.core bundle. For the comment, type:&#10;&#10;StrongPool is the default implementation of IPool. IPool is intended to be used through StrongPool and WeakPool, so the leak is intentional.&#10;&#10;I still don't know why API Tools treats IPool as non-API, so a filter hides the warning without fixing the cause. I think hiding it is acceptable here: IPool and StrongPool are in the same exported package, and the warning is pre-existing and unrelated to your branch. If the build doesn't fail on it, you can also skip it.&#10;&#10;how to resolve this&#10;This is the same warning as for StrongPool. WeakPool also implements IPool, and it needs its own filter.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'WeakPool'&quot;, in the p2.core bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;WeakPool is the default implementation of IPool. IPool is intended to be used through StrongPool and WeakPool, so the leak is intentional.&#10;&#10;The same caveat applies: this hides the warning without explaining why API Tools sees IPool as non-API.&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class EverythingOptionalProfile&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT pattern as before. EverythingOptionalProfile is a private nested class in SimplePlanner that implements IProfile. IProfile is @noimplement in p2.engine. The class is p2's own code, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'EverythingOptionalProfile'&quot;, in the p2.director bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;EverythingOptionalProfile is an internal wrapper used by SimplePlanner and intentionally implements IProfileThis is the same @noreference warning as the Projector ones, but in a different class. Slicer calls IRequirementChange.newValue() inside getRequirements(IInstallableUnit). Slicer is p2's own resolver code, so the call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'getRequirements(IInstallableUnit)'&quot;. Do it in the bundle that contains Slicer, which is probably p2.director. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;Slicer.getRequirements intentionally calls IRequirementChange.newValue(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own resolver&#10;&#10;If Slicer also calls other @noreference methods such as applyOn() or matches(...), each will need its own filter.&#10;&#10;▸ 1 earlier step&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class CachingArtifactRepository&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT pattern again. CachingArtifactRepository implements IFileArtifactRepository, which is @noimplement in p2.repository. It's p2's own code, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'CachingArtifactRepository'&quot;. This one goes in the p2.directorywatcher bundle, not p2.artifact.repository. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;CachingArtifactRepository is an internal wrapper used by the directory watcher and intentionally implements IFileArtifactRepository&#10;&#10;Also, Slicer from your last screenshot is in p2.director (Slicer.java), the same bundle as Projector. Its getRequirements filter goes in that bundle's .api_filters.&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class MissingActionsException|class ProvisionException|@noextend&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the extends version of the earlier warnings. The kind is ILLEGAL_EXTEND rather than ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT, but the cause is the same.&#10;&#10;MissingActionsException is an internal class in p2.engine. It extends ProvisionException from p2.core, which is @noextend (line 23). It's p2's own code, so subclassing it there is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'MissingActionsException'&quot;, in the p2.engine bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;MissingActionsException is an internal exception that intentionally extends ProvisionException&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class ExtensionLocationArtifactRepository&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT pattern as SimpleArtifactRepository and CachingArtifactRepository. ExtensionLocationArtifactRepository is in p2.extensionlocation. It implements IFileArtifactRepository, which is @noimplement in p2.repository. It's p2's own class, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ExtensionLocationArtifactRepository'&quot;, in the p2.extensionlocation bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ExtensionLocationArtifactRepository is the internal artifact repository for extension locations and intentionally implements IFileArtifactRepository&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class CompositeMetadataRepository&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT pattern as CompositeArtifactRepository. CompositeMetadataRepository implements ICompositeRepository&lt;IInstallableUnit&gt;, which is @noimplement in p2.repository. It's p2's own class, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'CompositeMetadataRepository'&quot;, in the p2.metadata.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;CompositeMetadataRepository is the internal default implementation of ICompositeRepository for metadata&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class MetadataWriter|getApplicabilityScope\(\)&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same ILLEGAL_REFERENCE (@noreference) pattern as the Projector and Slicer warnings. MetadataWriter calls IInstallableUnitPatch.getApplicabilityScope(), which is marked @noreference for clients. The writer is p2's own code that serializes patches, so the call is intentional. The quick fix is scoped to the method writeInstallableUnit(IInstallableUnit).&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'writeInstallableUnit(IInstallableUnit)'&quot;, in the p2.metadata.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;MetadataWriter.writeInstallableUnit intentionally calls IInstallableUnitPatch.getApplicabilityScope(), which is @noreference for clients but is needed to serialize patches&#10;&#10;getApplicabilityScope() is also called at line 133, which may be in a different method. If it is, you'll get a second warning that needs its own filter.This is another @noreference warning in MetadataWriter, in a different method. It's for IRequirementChange.applyOn() inside writeRequirementChange(IRequirementChange). It needs its own filter, separate from the writeInstallableUnit one.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'writeRequirementChange(IRequirementChange)'&quot;, in the p2.metadata.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;MetadataWriter.writeRequirementChange intentionally calls IRequirementChange.applyOn(), which is @noreference for clients but is needed to serialize requirement changes&#10;&#10;If writeRequirementChange also calls newValue(), expect another warning for it that needs its own filter.This is the second warning I expected for writeRequirementChange. It's for IRequirementChange.newValue(), in the same method as the applyOn() one. It needs its own filter, because each referenced method is reported separately.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'writeRequirementChange(IRequirementChange)'&quot;, in the p2.metadata.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;MetadataWriter.writeRequirementChange intentionally calls IRequirementChange.newValue(), which is @noreference for clients but is needed to serialize requirement changesThis is the same ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT pattern as ArtifactRepositoryManager. MetadataRepositoryManager implements IMetadataRepositoryManager, which is @noimplement in p2.repository (line 29). I noted this earlier when I compared it with the artifact repository manager. It's p2's own class, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'MetadataRepositoryManager'&quot;, in the p2.metadata.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;MetadataRepositoryManager is the internal default implementation of IMetadataRepositoryManager&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class ExpressionParser|interface IExpressionConstants&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the API_LEAK kind, like the StrongPool and WeakPool warnings. ExpressionParser implements IExpressionConstants. Both are internal classes in the p2.metadata bundle. The constants interface is only used as a constants holder, so the leak is harmless.&#10;&#10;I haven't worked out why API Tools reports this as a leak, so a filter hides the warning without fixing the cause.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ExpressionParser'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ExpressionParser implements the internal IExpressionConstants only to share constants, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;interface IContextExpression|interface IExpression\b&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the API_LEAK kind again, like StrongPool and WeakPool. IContextExpression extends IExpression. Both are in the same package in p2.metadata.&#10;&#10;IExpression is a public interface in an API package, which is the same oddity as IPool. I haven't worked out why API Tools calls it non-API, so a filter hides the warning without fixing the cause.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'IContextExpression'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;IContextExpression intentionally extends IExpression, which is part of the p2 expression API, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;interface IFilterExpression&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is another warning of the same leak family. IFilterExpression extends IExpression and also the OSGi Filter. The wording differs from the last one (&quot;leaks APIs from the superinterface&quot;), but the cause is the same: it's in the same package in p2.metadata and builds on IExpression. It's a deliberate part of p2's expression API.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'IFilterExpression'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;IFilterExpression intentionally extends IExpression as part of the p2 expression API, so the leak is intentionalThis is the same IExpression leak warning as IContextExpression. IMatchExpression&lt;T&gt; extends IExpression in the same package in p2.metadata, and the relationship is deliberate.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'IMatchExpression'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;IMatchExpression intentionally extends IExpression as part of the p2 expression API, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;interface IQueryWithIndex&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same API_LEAK warning as the IExpression ones. IQueryWithIndex extends IQuery&lt;T&gt;, which is @noimplement and @noextend in IQuery.java. Both are in the p2.metadata bundle, and the extension is part of p2's own query API.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'IQueryWithIndex'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;IQueryWithIndex intentionally extends IQuery as part of the p2 query API, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class ExpressionMatchQuery|interface IMatchQuery&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same API_LEAK family. ExpressionMatchQuery implements IMatchQuery&lt;T&gt;, which is @noimplement and builds on IQuery. Both are in p2.metadata, and the class is p2's own implementation.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ExpressionMatchQuery'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ExpressionMatchQuery is p2's own implementation of IMatchQuery, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;ExpressionMatchQuery also implements IQueryWithIndex&lt;T&gt;. If that causes a second warning, it needs its own filter.This is the same warning as for ExpressionMatchQuery. MatchQuery&lt;T&gt; is another p2 class implementing IMatchQuery&lt;T&gt;, so the leak is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'MatchQuery'&quot;, in the p2.metadata bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;MatchQuery is p2's own base implementation of IMatchQuery, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;▸ 2 earlier steps&#10;EXECUTE&#10;grep -n &quot;class ProfileChangeOperation&quot; -B6 bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.operations/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/operations/ProfileChangeOperation.java; grep -n &quot;Export-Package&quot; -A6 bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.operations/META-INF/MANIFEST.MF&#10;This is the same API_LEAK family, but it's in a bundle your branch touches. RemediationOperation extends ProfileChangeOperation. That class is @noextend for clients and implements IProfileChangeJob, which is @noimplement and @noextend.&#10;&#10;Your diff to RemediationOperation doesn't change the class declaration. The extends ProfileChangeOperation line is unchanged, so I don't think your edits caused this warning.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'RemediationOperation'&quot;, in the p2.operations bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;RemediationOperation intentionally extends ProfileChangeOperation as part of the p2 operations API, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;The existing .api_filters already has an entry for RemediationOperation, for getOriginalRequest(). The quick fix will add a second filter to that same &lt;resource&gt; block.&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class IUAnalyzer|interface IIUAnalyzer&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same API_LEAK kind. IUAnalyzer is an abstract base class that implements IIUAnalyzer. IIUAnalyzer is @noimplement and @noextend, and the javadoc tells clients to subclass IUAnalyzer instead. Both are in p2.repository.tools, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'IUAnalyzer'&quot;, in the p2.repository.tools bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;IUAnalyzer is the intended base class for clients and intentionally implements IIUAnalyzer, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class AbstractArtifactRepository&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same API_LEAK kind. AbstractArtifactRepository implements IArtifactRepository, which is @noimplement and @noextend. The javadoc on IArtifactRepository tells clients to subclass AbstractArtifactRepository instead, so the implementation is intentional. Both are in p2.repository.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'AbstractArtifactRepository'&quot;, in the p2.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;AbstractArtifactRepository is the intended base class for clients and intentionally implements IArtifactRepository, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class ArtifactDescriptor &quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the same API_LEAK kind. ArtifactDescriptor implements IArtifactDescriptor, which is @noimplement and @noextend. The javadoc on IArtifactDescriptor tells clients to subclass ArtifactDescriptor instead, so the implementation is intentional. Both are in p2.repository.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ArtifactDescriptor'&quot;, in the p2.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ArtifactDescriptor is the intended base class for clients and intentionally implements IArtifactDescriptor, so the leak is intentionalThis is the same API_LEAK kind. ProcessingStepDescriptor implements IProcessingStepDescriptor, which is @noimplement and @noextend in p2.repository. The javadoc on the interface tells clients to subclass ProcessingStepDescriptor instead, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'ProcessingStepDescriptor'&quot;, in the p2.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ProcessingStepDescriptor is the intended base class for clients and intentionally implements IProcessingStepDescriptor, so the leak is intentionalThis is the same API_LEAK kind. AbstractMetadataRepository implements IMetadataRepository, which is @noimplement in p2.repository. The javadoc on IMetadataRepository tells clients to subclass AbstractMetadataRepository instead (line 31), so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'AbstractMetadataRepository'&quot;, in the p2.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;AbstractMetadataRepository is the intended base class for clients and intentionally implements IMetadataRepository, so the leak is intentionalThis is the same API_LEAK kind. AbstractRepository&lt;T&gt; implements IRepository&lt;T&gt;, which is @noimplement in p2.repository. The javadoc on IRepository says to use the abstract classes implementing it instead (line 30), so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'AbstractRepository'&quot;, in the p2.repository bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;AbstractRepository is the intended base class for clients and intentionally implements IRepository, so the leak is intentional&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class AggregatedBundleRepository&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the ILLEGAL_IMPLEMENT kind, the same as SimpleArtifactRepository. AggregatedBundleRepository implements IFileArtifactRepository, which is @noimplement in p2.repository. It's p2's own internal class, so the implementation is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'AggregatedBundleRepository'&quot;, in the p2.touchpoint.eclipse bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;AggregatedBundleRepository is an internal repository used by the Eclipse touchpoint and intentionally implements IFileArtifactRepository&#10;&#10;▸ 1 earlier step&#10;EXECUTE&#10;sed -n 440,448p bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.ui/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/ui/ProvisioningUI.java; grep -n &quot;RepositoriesView\|ProfilesView&quot; bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.ui.admin/.settings/.api_filters 2&gt;/dev/null | head&#10;This is the ILLEGAL_REFERENCE (@noreference) kind, like the Projector ones. ProfilesView calls ProvisioningUI.getOperationRunner() inside addListeners(). That method is marked @noreference (ProvisioningUI.java) and described as for automated testing only. ProfilesView is p2's own admin UI, so the call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'addListeners()'&quot;, in the p2.ui.admin bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ProfilesView.addListeners intentionally calls ProvisioningUI.getOperationRunner(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own admin UI&#10;&#10;RepositoriesView calls the same method (line 107). It will give a separate warning that needs its own filter in the same bundle.This is the RepositoriesView warning I expected: the same @noreference call to ProvisioningUI.getOperationRunner() as in ProfilesView. The quick fix is scoped to the anonymous class new StructuredViewerProvisioningListener() {...}, because the call at line 107 creates that listener.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'new StructuredViewerProvisioningListener() {...}'&quot;, in the p2.ui.admin bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;RepositoriesView intentionally calls ProvisioningUI.getOperationRunner(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own admin UI&#10;&#10;▸ 1 earlier step&#10;EXECUTE&#10;sed -n 30,42p bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.operations/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/operations/RemediationOperation.java; git diff origin/master HEAD -- bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.operations/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/operations/RemediationOperation.java | grep -n &quot;noreference\|noextend\|noinstantiate&quot;; git show origin/master:bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.operations/src/org/eclipse/equinox/p2/operations/RemediationOperation.java | sed -n 36,42p&#10;This is the ILLEGAL_REFERENCE (@noreference) kind, like the earlier ones. RemediationOperation is marked @noreference at class level in its javadoc (line 38), so any use from another bundle gets flagged. The tag is in origin/master too, so it isn't something your branch added. DiscoveryInstallOperation is p2's own code creating RemediationOperation inside run(IProgressMonitor), so the reference is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'run(IProgressMonitor)'&quot;, in the p2.ui.discovery bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;DiscoveryInstallOperation.run intentionally creates a RemediationOperation, which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI&#10;&#10;Other p2 callers create RemediationOperation too: UpdateHandler in p2.ui.sdk, and UpdateAction and ProvisioningOperationWizard in p2.ui. Each may give a similar warning that needs its own filter in its own bundle.This is another @noreference warning in the same method, and it needs its own filter. The earlier one was for the RemediationOperation constructor. This one is for ProfileChangeOperation.resolveModal(IProgressMonitor), which DiscoveryInstallOperation.run calls on the operation it creates. The call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'run(IProgressMonitor)'&quot;, in the p2.ui.discovery bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;DiscoveryInstallOperation.run intentionally calls ProfileChangeOperation.resolveModal(IProgressMonitor), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI&#10;&#10;If run calls other @noreference methods on RemediationOperation, each one will need its own filter too.This is the ILLEGAL_EXTEND kind, like the MissingActionsException one. ImportWizard.recomputePlan(IRunnableContext, boolean) creates an anonymous subclass of InstallOperation. InstallOperation is @noextend in p2.operations (line 50). ImportWizard is p2's own import/export UI, so subclassing it there is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'new InstallOperation() {...}'&quot;, in the p2.ui.importexport bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;ImportWizard.recomputePlan intentionally subclasses InstallOperation, which is @noextend for clients but is used by p2's own import/export UIThis is the warning I mentioned for UpdateHandler. It's the same @noreference constructor warning as DiscoveryInstallOperation, but this one is the three-argument constructor RemediationOperation(ProvisioningSession, IProfileChangeRequest, RemedyConfig[]). UpdateHandler is p2's own SDK UI, so the reference is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'doExecute(LoadMetadataRepositoryJob)'&quot;, in the p2.ui.sdk bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateHandler.doExecute intentionally creates a RemediationOperation, which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own SDK UI&#10;&#10;UpdateAction and ProvisioningOperationWizard in p2.ui create RemediationOperation too. They will give their own warnings in that bundle.This is another @noreference warning in the same method as the last one, so it needs its own filter. UpdateHandler.doExecute calls RemedyConfig.getCheckForUpdateRemedyConfigs() when it builds the RemediationOperation. RemedyConfig is part of the same experimental remediation API, so the call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'doExecute(LoadMetadataRepositoryJob)'&quot;, in the p2.ui.sdk bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateHandler.doExecute intentionally calls RemedyConfig.getCheckForUpdateRemedyConfigs(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own SDK UI&#10;&#10;Also expect a warning for UpdateHandler.doExecute referencing the RemedyConfig type itself.&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.ui.sdk for &quot;getAllRemedyConfigs|runModal|RemedyConfig&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is another @noreference warning from UpdateHandler. It's in a different place from the last two, so it needs its own filter. UpdateHandler calls RemedyConfig.getAllRemedyConfigs() inside the anonymous job's runModal(IProgressMonitor), to size the progress monitor. It's p2's own SDK UI, so the call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'runModal(IProgressMonitor)'&quot;, in the p2.ui.sdk bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateHandler's runModal intentionally calls RemedyConfig.getAllRemedyConfigs(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own SDK UIThis is another @noreference warning in the same method as the last one, so it needs its own filter. It's for ProfileChangeOperation.resolveModal(IProgressMonitor), which UpdateHandler's runModal(IProgressMonitor) calls on the RemediationOperation. The call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'runModal(IProgressMonitor)'&quot;, in the p2.ui.sdk bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateHandler's runModal intentionally calls ProfileChangeOperation.resolveModal(IProgressMonitor), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own SDK UI&#10;&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles for &quot;class PropertyDialogAction&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is the ILLEGAL_EXTEND kind. PropertyDialogAction in p2.ui extends org.eclipse.ui.dialogs.PropertyDialogAction from the Eclipse UI workbench. That class is marked @noextend there. p2 subclasses it on purpose to customize the properties dialog.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'PropertyDialogAction'&quot;, in the p2.ui bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;p2's PropertyDialogAction intentionally extends org.eclipse.ui.dialogs.PropertyDialogAction to customize the properties dialog for p2 elements&#10;&#10;This one comes from the Eclipse UI bundle, not from p2. I haven't checked how p2 customizes the dialog, so the comment is based only on the class name and where it extends from. If you know the real reason, use that instead.&#10;&#10;Pasted image&#10;This is the UpdateAction warning I mentioned earlier. It's the same @noreference constructor warning as DiscoveryInstallOperation and UpdateHandler, but for the two-argument constructor RemediationOperation(ProvisioningSession, IProfileChangeRequest). UpdateAction is p2's own UI, so the reference is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'performAction(ProfileChangeOperation, Collection&lt;IInstallableUnit&gt;)'&quot;, in the p2.ui bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateAction.performAction intentionally creates a RemediationOperation, which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI&#10;&#10;UpdateAction may also give warnings for other @noreference calls on RemediationOperation in the same method, such as resolveModal. Each would need its own filter.&#10;&#10;Pasted image&#10;This is the same screenshot as the last one: the UpdateAction constructor reference to RemediationOperation(ProvisioningSession, IProfileChangeRequest). If you've already applied that filter, the warning should be gone, so rebuild and check whether this one is stale.&#10;&#10;If it's still showing, pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'performAction(ProfileChangeOperation, Collection&lt;IInstallableUnit&gt;)'&quot;, in the p2.ui bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateAction.performAction intentionally creates a RemediationOperation, which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI&#10;&#10;Check .api_filters first, so you don't add a duplicate entry for the same method.&#10;&#10;Pasted image&#10;READ&#10;Searching files in /Users/subyssurendran/equinox/git/p2/bundles/org.eclipse.equinox.p2.ui for &quot;getAllRemedyConfigs|runModal|resolveModal|RemedyConfig&quot;&#10;Compare / Diff&#10;This is another @noreference warning from UpdateAction, and it needs its own filter. UpdateAction calls RemedyConfig.getAllRemedyConfigs() inside the anonymous job's runModal(IProgressMonitor), to size the progress monitor. It's the same pattern as UpdateHandler, and the call is intentional.&#10;&#10;Pick &quot;Create a commented usage problem filter for 'runModal(IProgressMonitor)'&quot;, in the p2.ui bundle. Use this comment:&#10;&#10;UpdateAction's runModal intentionally calls RemedyConfig.getAllRemedyConfigs(), which is @noreference for clients but is used by p2's own UI" id="640712815">
            <message_arguments>
                <message_argument value="RemedyConfig"/>
                <message_argument value="UpdateAction"/>
                <message_argument value="getAllRemedyConfigs()"/>
            </message_arguments>
        </filter>
